WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.820
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.920
It is the former National Security Advisor

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.320
to President Trump and
the former US Ambassador

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.320
to the United Nations, John Bolton.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.025
Reagan, the two Bush's, then Donald Trump,

00:00:14.025 --> 00:00:17.130
fewer people have had a better
seat to the changing face

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.070
of the Republican party.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.900
Since the 80s, Bolton has
been a hawkish influence

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.002
in the party's foreign policy circles.

00:00:24.002 --> 00:00:24.940
Over time,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.663
he has gone from background bureaucrat

00:00:26.663 --> 00:00:28.688
to a more visible presence,

00:00:28.688 --> 00:00:32.150
loyally serving former
President George W. Bush

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.470
and later joining the
Trump administration.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.260
He made some very big
mistakes when he talked

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.950
about the Libyan model for Kim Jong-un,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.803
that was not a good statement to make,

00:00:43.803 --> 00:00:46.330
but he's somebody that I

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.780
actually had a very
good relationship with,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.590
but he wasn't getting along with people

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.570
in the administration that
I consider very important.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.360
Bolton's tell-all memoir released in 2020,

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.190
showed disdain for his
colleagues in the administration,

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.960
but his failure to testify
before the House Committee

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.080
during Trump's impeachment has led

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.863
to criticism that he was
silent when it mattered most.

00:01:07.863 --> 00:01:10.780
He tells me why he thinks President Biden

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.227
has failed the Ukrainian people.

00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:15.730
He recalls a meeting he had with Putin

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.150
and why he has zero regrets

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.910
about not testifying
against his former boss,

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.030
President Trump.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.730
If your previous boss, President Trump,

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.330
was in charge at the moment,

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:28.750
where do you think we would be right now?

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.360
I think the Russian forces
would be in Kyiv by now,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.750
but saying Trump is deficient
is an easy thing to do.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:37.400
I do it quite frequently.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.410
The real problem is you've only
got one President at a time

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.730
and we are in a situation now
where we have the perception

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.120
in Europe, and in much of Congress,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.480
that the leadership is weak,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.250
that it's being dragged
along as events unfold,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.023
rather than really trying to
shape the overall environment.

00:02:08.989 --> 00:02:11.470
Thank you very much for joining me today.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.063
It's very good to talk to you.

00:02:13.063 --> 00:02:15.930
I wanted to begin by talking
about foreign policy,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:16.920
America's foreign policy,

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.510
because President Biden
talked about being back

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.490
on the international diplomatic stage

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.290
after the Trump years.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.365
It's safe to say you're
no fan of President Trump.

00:02:26.365 --> 00:02:28.510
Is the foreign policy now better

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.670
than what America had directly before?

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.680
Well, it's like being asked

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.610
to judge a harmonica concert, really.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.848
I mean the Biden approach is
very different than Trump's,

00:02:37.848 --> 00:02:41.830
Biden has thought about
foreign policy extensively

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:45.450
over a long career, but in many respects,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.960
his conclusions are wrong.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.790
So it's the difference
between somebody pursuing

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:54.160
a generally incorrect line
in foreign policy compared

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.760
to another President
who had no line at all.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.170
In the sense though,

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.770
of where we are specifically with Ukraine,

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.147
do you feel he's getting that right?

00:03:03.147 --> 00:03:05.025
Not well enough.

00:03:05.025 --> 00:03:09.890
And as the saying goes, close
is only good in horseshoes.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:13.490
I don't think that the
NATO Alliance is as united

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:14.880
as he says it is.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.475
I don't think the response is effective

00:03:17.475 --> 00:03:20.670
and the greatest tragedy of
all, the biggest failure,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:25.360
a failure we live through
every day is the failure

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.200
to deter the Russian
invasion to begin with.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.952
So when you look at it
from that perspective,

00:03:30.952 --> 00:03:35.620
there's a lot of rhetoric
that makes people feel warm

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.980
and fuzzy, but it didn't translate
into an effective policy.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.150
And it's still not translating
into an effective policy.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.350
Well, forgive me though, but there's a lot

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.750
of money that's being spent...

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.260
Well, wait a minute, forgive me.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.370
People say, well, we've
committed X billion dollars,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.900
or we're pledged to
give this weapon system.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.540
This is government talk.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.625
This is the talk of inputs.

00:03:58.625 --> 00:04:02.660
The question, particularly
in a war, is outputs.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.452
And we can certainly say
that the Ukrainian army

00:04:05.452 --> 00:04:08.223
has fought bravely and quite well.

00:04:08.223 --> 00:04:11.900
But the issue now is
whether all of the rhetoric,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.380
all of the commitments that
different NATO members have made

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.470
are actually translated into reality.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.540
And that remains to be seen.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.300
But in terms of the
money that has been spent

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.780
at the time we're
talking, it's 3.65 billion

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.963
for weapon transfers and sales to Ukraine.

00:04:27.415 --> 00:04:28.538
If I may finish.

00:04:28.538 --> 00:04:30.512
It's another 3 billion...
It's announced.

00:04:30.512 --> 00:04:32.588
You don't think that's actually happened?

00:04:32.588 --> 00:04:35.162
Do you believe that $3.6 billion

00:04:35.162 --> 00:04:38.090
of weapons have crossed
the Ukrainian frontier

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:39.650
and are now in Ukraine?

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.607
I have no answer to that or no expertise.

00:04:41.607 --> 00:04:43.400
But that's the point, isn't it?

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.277
We're in a race for time.

00:04:45.277 --> 00:04:48.840
Russia's military performance
has been appalling.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.380
And the point that we're at now is

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:55.860
whether we get weapons and
munitions to the Ukrainian army

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.667
before Putin and the Russians can regroup

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:02.817
and achieve their objectives
and that remains to be seen.

00:05:02.817 --> 00:05:05.833
But on what basis do you
not believe that that sort

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:07.870
of support has actually got there?

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.240
That's what I'm trying to understand.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.350
Well, among other reasons,

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.270
because that's what the Ukrainians say.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.410
And because that's the
way government works,

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.817
it's very easy for political
leaders to make commitments.

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:21.430
It's quite another thing to deliver them

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.420
to the troops in the front lines.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:26.700
When somebody says we've
delivered $3.2 billion,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.400
you yourself confess, you don't know

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.950
where the resources that
were to be purchased

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.530
with that money, where they are.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.870
Are they in Poland or are they in Ukraine?

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:38.150
I suppose what I wanted to understand is,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.900
do you think at the moment
that if that hasn't got there,

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.580
if all that money and all that
weaponry hasn't got there,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.980
what else do you think
America should be doing?

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.300
Well, I think the question
of giving resources

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.590
to Ukraine has to depend fundamentally

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.003
on what the strategic objective is.

00:05:56.003 --> 00:06:00.170
And I don't think after
having failed tragically,

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:04.468
catastrophically, failed to
deter the Russian invasion,

00:06:04.468 --> 00:06:08.190
it has not been at all
clear what the American

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.690
or NATO strategic objective is.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:15.093
I think it should have been
for Ukraine to win the war.

00:06:15.093 --> 00:06:19.980
I think it was a mistake
to believe, clearly,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:23.360
we made intelligence mistakes
just as the Russians did

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.460
about what the outcome
and the opening weeks

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:26.882
of the war would be.

00:06:26.882 --> 00:06:30.440
But there has been really no replacement

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:34.070
for a strategic objective
we should be working toward.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:37.793
And I think without that,
whatever the level of effort,

00:06:37.793 --> 00:06:42.793
it's not likely to be
sufficient to achieve an outcome

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.930
that will deter the Russians
in the future and deter others

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.590
because China in particular
is watching what's happening

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:54.680
in Ukraine very carefully
and adjusting their policies

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.690
along their periphery in the
Indo-Pacific accordingly.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:02.505
So lack of strategic focus,
lack of delivery on rhetoric,

00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:04.230
on the weapons front,

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:07.310
and lack of delivery on
the sanctions front too.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:08.480
Do you want boots on the ground?

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.040
Do you want American boots in that?

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.590
I think we've passed the
point where that's possible.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:14.500
So the question is, for example,

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.630
when will Germany, Austria,
Italy, the Netherlands,

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:21.490
cease their reliance
on Russian oil and gas?

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:24.995
Right now, people estimate
the cost of the war

00:07:24.995 --> 00:07:29.995
to Russia is being exceeded by
the cost of European payments

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:34.230
to Russia in hard currency
for the continued delivery

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:35.682
of energy supplies.

00:07:35.682 --> 00:07:40.210
So that on net, Putin is still
making a profit off this war.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:43.580
I don't consider that to be
an example of allied unity.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:46.082
It's gone past the point
of a military intervention,

00:07:46.082 --> 00:07:47.180
as you see it?

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.140
Given that Joe Biden is President.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:52.410
I think this has been a
failure of American leadership

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:53.970
in many respects.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:56.570
What we've seen is a
continual modification

00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:00.180
of the American position
getting stronger and stronger.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.690
Would you have supported
boots on the ground

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:02.828
at the beginning?

00:08:02.828 --> 00:08:05.590
I would've long before this,

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.700
supported putting more
Americans and NATO forces

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.732
on the ground to train and
assist Ukrainian forces

00:08:12.732 --> 00:08:16.110
so that Russian generals looking
through their field glasses

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:19.070
across the border would've
seen a lot of American flags

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.830
and wondered what it meant.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:24.610
Instead in early December, Biden,

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:28.820
without anything in exchange
from the Russians on his own,

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.640
ruled out the use of American troops.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:32.750
He didn't have to do that.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:34.300
That was an unforced error.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.250
That allowed Putin to
check that off his list.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.120
What Biden could have done instead,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.440
could have done two things.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.430
One, he just could have remained silent,

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:47.300
or he could have said that
all options were on the table.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.632
And in both of these other options,

00:08:49.632 --> 00:08:53.145
it would've put the burden
of ambiguity on Putin,

00:08:53.145 --> 00:08:55.500
left him something to worry about,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.860
added to the potential deterrence,

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.310
but instead by taking it off the table,

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:02.240
Biden took it out of
the deterrence calculus.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.340
Even if you accept some of those missteps,

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.260
regardless of where you are
on the political divide,

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.100
there are many who feel uncomfortable

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:10.730
about boots on the ground because

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:14.240
of the escalation that
they fear with Russia.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:16.950
If your previous boss, President Trump,

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.260
for whom you were National
Security Advisor, of course,

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:20.918
was in charge at the moment,

00:09:20.918 --> 00:09:23.260
where do you think we would be right now?

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.818
I think the Russian forces
would be in Kyiv by now,

00:09:25.818 --> 00:09:30.300
but saying Trump is deficient
is an easy thing to do.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.950
I do it quite frequently.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:36.243
The real problem is you've only
got one President at a time.

00:09:36.243 --> 00:09:40.340
And we are in a situation now
where we have the perception

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.690
in Europe and in much of Congress

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.090
that the leadership is weak,

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:47.850
that it's being dragged
along as events unfold

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.530
rather than really trying to
shape the overall environment.

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:56.515
Had there been a more effective
effort before those comments

00:09:56.515 --> 00:09:59.750
of Biden's in December,

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.470
we might have been able
to deter the invasion

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:04.300
to begin with, that was the objective.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.620
And that failure is something
we see now every day,

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.150
but ironically, whereas
NATO and the US failed

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:12.490
to deter Putin,

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.480
we're now being deterred by Putin.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.480
And I think that, as you said,
the fear of escalation works

00:10:19.572 --> 00:10:21.560
to Putin's advantage.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.820
And what that means in practice is

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.920
that Ukraine is being
ground into the dust.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:28.860
Can I just ask about
something that's been said

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.700
by a former National
Security Council Official,

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:33.290
Alexander Vindman.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.880
He said that the January 6th attack

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:39.670
on the Capitol was a signal
to Putin to start preparing

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:41.200
to invade Ukraine.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:42.465
Do you buy that link?

00:10:42.465 --> 00:10:46.910
No, I don't because I don't
think in Trump's mind,

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:49.950
it was anything more
complicated than trying

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:52.900
to prevent the counting of
the electoral college vote.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:55.280
And Trump was on his way out of office.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.217
He went out of office, he was
replaced by another President.

00:10:58.217 --> 00:11:00.740
Putin had met Biden before,

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:02.670
he met him again in Europe last summer

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:04.350
for three and a half hours,

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:07.618
he took Biden's measure
and then he prepared steps

00:11:07.618 --> 00:11:09.450
to go to war.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:12.220
So I think it was a
calculation in Putin's mind,

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:14.680
based on the President
sitting in front of him.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.530
It's only because there is that concern

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:17.550
about that potential link,

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.960
so it's interesting to
hear your take on it

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:21.193
with regards to-

00:11:21.193 --> 00:11:23.140
Let me be clear, let me be clear.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:25.170
Trump's not smart enough
to make that calculus.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:26.670
No, no, no, Putin is looking at it.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:28.790
It's about Putin's perception,
not what Trump did.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:30.110
Yeah but that's fine.

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:31.910
But Putin was at that point,

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:34.640
looking at a President with
14 days left in office.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.180
Do you think Ukraine will be victorious?

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:38.013
Well, I hope so

00:11:38.013 --> 00:11:40.130
because I think peace
and security in Europe,

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.730
which should be of highest
concern to Europeans

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.900
in the first instance,
will be badly damaged

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:48.500
if Russia has military success here,

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:51.760
I think that's still
very much to be decided.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:52.790
I think their performance

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.452
to date has been incredibly ineffective,

00:11:56.452 --> 00:11:59.560
but if at some point Putin

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.840
with a straight face can
declare a military victory,

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:06.340
I think the architecture of
Europe is now in question

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:07.750
for a long time to come.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:11.380
And I think Putin made
it very clear in 2005,

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.590
what he wanted to do
when he said the breakup

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:14.690
of the Soviet Union

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.000
was the greatest geopolitical catastrophe

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:18.590
of the 20th century.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:20.170
So I think what it says is

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:24.360
to all the former constituent
parts of the Soviet Union

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:26.310
that you could be next.

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:28.407
You actually met Putin, I understand.

00:12:28.407 --> 00:12:30.660
And I wonder how would you describe him?

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:33.210
There are many words
being used at the moment.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.460
Well, I think he's very coldblooded.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:36.690
I think he's calculating.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:38.720
I think he knows what
Russia's interests are

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:41.300
and I think he's prepared
to do almost anything.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:44.964
I don't buy the argument that
Putin's got a screw loose.

00:12:44.964 --> 00:12:48.470
That's convenient, but we
shouldn't personalize this war.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:52.539
It's not Putin's war alone,
large numbers of Russians,

00:12:52.539 --> 00:12:55.880
not simply because of propaganda,
but because of history,

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.910
think of Ukraine and Belarus
while we're on the subject,

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.664
is part of the Rodinia,
part of Mother Russia,

00:13:01.664 --> 00:13:03.353
and they want it back.

00:13:03.353 --> 00:13:05.730
It's not about, if I may,

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:09.160
it's not about personalizing,
it's about justice as well

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:12.150
and who will be held to account
and how this could play out

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:13.500
or how people want it to play out.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.580
I mean, he's now been
described as a war criminal

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:18.570
by Joe Biden, of course, and others,

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:22.270
there are now leaders calling
for him to be in the Hague.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:24.460
You were instrumental
in the US's withdrawal

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:27.200
from the International Criminal Court.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:28.600
What is your response to that?

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:30.170
That he should be in the Hague?

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:32.240
Well, I don't think anybody
should be in the Hague

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:35.510
because I think the ICC
is a fundamentally lawless

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:37.150
and illegitimate institution.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:40.720
I think the remedy here is
what Ukrainian prosecutors

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:43.013
and investigators have
already started to do,

00:13:43.013 --> 00:13:47.992
to prepare the basis for crimes committed

00:13:47.992 --> 00:13:50.717
against the Ukrainians over
the course of this war.

00:13:50.717 --> 00:13:52.750
I think that's the way to do it,

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.610
but I think it's important
for you to understand

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:57.501
and others who think like you do,

00:13:57.501 --> 00:14:00.460
that these threats of
war crimes prosecutions

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:03.617
do not deter Putin in the slightest

00:14:03.617 --> 00:14:05.880
and you can talk about them.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.160
I'm sure it makes you
feel all warm and fuzzy.

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:11.526
If possible.

00:14:11.526 --> 00:14:13.334
Could you stop personalizing this to me

00:14:13.334 --> 00:14:16.067
You have no idea what I
think, it's not relevant,

00:14:16.067 --> 00:14:17.010
I'm quoting leaders.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.310
You're making a big mistake
in your understanding

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.130
of who the adversary is.

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:23.320
Please don't make a mistake
of my personal views.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:25.140
I'm quoting world leaders.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.080
And when you say you need to understand

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.520
because you think like you do,
you have no idea how I think,

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.130
'cause it's not relevant to the interview.

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:31.963
What's relevant is...

00:14:31.963 --> 00:14:34.525
I think I get a pretty
good sense of it, honestly.

00:14:34.525 --> 00:14:36.130
Well I like facts.

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:37.860
So do I.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.690
With your credibility as an advisor,

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:43.150
former National Security
Advisor, you've held many roles.

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:44.680
You've also, of course, been an ambassador

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.520
to the United Nations.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:49.256
How much has your credibility
been hit by your decision

00:14:49.256 --> 00:14:52.257
to put your concerns about
President Trump in a book

00:14:52.257 --> 00:14:55.880
rather than testify before
Congress during his impeachment?

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:59.620
Well, I decided that the appropriate thing

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.960
to do when confronted with
the impeachment investigation,

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:05.333
which I thought was doomed to failure,

00:15:05.333 --> 00:15:08.600
was to make myself
available on the same basis

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:10.947
as every other witness who testified,

00:15:10.947 --> 00:15:13.180
pursuant to a subpoena.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.700
My deputy was subpoenaed.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:18.670
He tried to get an answer in
litigation when he got an order

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:20.800
from President Trump not to testify

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:23.670
and the House of
Representatives backed away.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:25.180
I was never subpoenaed.

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:27.730
I offered to testify to the Senate.

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:30.010
They decided they didn't want evidence.

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:33.420
I did the best I could
under the circumstances.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.640
And I think the effort to produce the book

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:41.280
to give the full story of what
happened is the best thing

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:42.113
to have done.

00:15:42.113 --> 00:15:46.120
And I did it based on my view
of what the right approach was

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:48.250
and answer to my own conscience.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:49.572
Do you regret it?

00:15:49.572 --> 00:15:52.580
No, it was a difficult decision.

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:55.069
It was a hard situation,

00:15:55.069 --> 00:15:59.250
but I thought about it
as carefully as I could.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.990
I tried to do what I thought was right.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:03.220
And I feel comfortable with the decision.

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:05.460
It's just because you have alienated some

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:07.400
in your own party, many in your own party,

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:08.610
of course the other side were never

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:11.360
probably the biggest
fans, it's safe to say,

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:13.221
party politics being what it is.

00:16:13.221 --> 00:16:16.280
And some on your own side,
in the Republican side,

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:17.840
that led to you being called the thing

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:19.930
that I'm sure you would resist very hard,

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.500
which is unpatriotic.

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.910
Look, you can only say to
thine own self be true.

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:26.000
I did the best I could.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:28.220
So your version is you weren't asked,

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:29.860
you weren't subpoenaed to testify

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:31.200
and then you weren't asked,

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:33.330
so you didn't testify so
instead, you put it in a book.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.080
Well, it's much more complicated.

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:37.530
So if you want the full story,
as I said, at the beginning,

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:41.340
I saw this impeachment
effort as basically doomed

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.660
to failure and it did fail.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.100
Trump was acquitted.

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:49.340
So my concern was that
in the way the advocates

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.235
of impeachment were proceeding,

00:16:51.235 --> 00:16:53.380
they were dooming themselves

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:56.730
to an outcome where
Trump would be acquitted.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:00.610
And so completely the
opposite of their objective

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:04.130
to punish Trump in the
best outcome or at least

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.330
to constrain and deter him,

00:17:06.330 --> 00:17:08.850
they achieved exactly the opposite.

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:11.070
They emboldened him, they enabled him.

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:13.870
They allowed him to say,
I got away with it once,

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:15.690
maybe I can get away with it again.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:18.960
I thought the entire effort
was doomed to failure.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:21.000
And I thought the best thing I could do

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.890
in those circumstances
when they were guilty

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:25.700
of impeachment malpractice,

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:28.060
as we can see in retrospect,

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:31.797
to tell the story in its fullest detail.

00:17:31.797 --> 00:17:32.660
You could have done both.

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:35.220
And get it out there and
allow people to read it.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.870
They can read the book and they
can make up their own mind.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:39.170
Couldn't you have done both?

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:43.196
I've been involved in American
politics for a long time.

00:17:43.196 --> 00:17:46.250
In the entire history
of the United States,

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:49.550
there's only been one
successful impeachment

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:51.160
and it wasn't even through impeachment,

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:53.760
but through the resignation
of Richard Nixon.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:58.760
And the way that occurred
was the growing feeling

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:02.130
on a bipartisan basis,
that Nixon had to go.

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:03.600
The advocates of impeachment

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:06.657
against Trump rejected the possibility

00:18:06.657 --> 00:18:09.780
of getting Republican sympathy.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.730
They got a very limited number of votes,

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:16.330
no possible way that they
would get to two thirds

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:18.084
of the votes in the Senate,

00:18:18.084 --> 00:18:20.240
the way they conducted the affair.

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.360
And I do think that was
entirely predictable,

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:23.740
right from the get go.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:25.110
I think it's just people
are very uncomfortable

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.770
with the idea of somebody
profiting from that sort

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:29.610
of description as well.

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:32.847
Well, look, there's a long history

00:18:32.847 --> 00:18:36.859
of American officials
writing their memoirs.

00:18:36.859 --> 00:18:40.050
And many people have written their memoirs

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:42.480
of the Trump administration,
both those for Trump

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.111
and those against Trump.

00:18:44.111 --> 00:18:47.733
And that's a very, very common practice.

00:18:47.733 --> 00:18:50.160
It is, but there is a particular context

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:51.680
which we've explored at length.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:52.590
Do you have guilt,

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:55.750
having been President Trump's
National Security Advisor?

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.090
No, I don't have a sense of guilt.

00:18:57.090 --> 00:18:59.110
I took the job with an effort

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:02.390
to enhance American national security.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:06.240
I felt having heard everything
there was to hear about Trump

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:10.300
before I took the job, that nonetheless,

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.222
like every other American President,

00:19:12.222 --> 00:19:15.130
that the gravity of the responsibility,

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:17.120
the weight of the decisions he would have

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:18.950
to make would discipline him,

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:21.297
at least in the national security field.

00:19:21.297 --> 00:19:24.150
And it turned out I was
wrong in that assessment,

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:27.947
but the effort that I
spent over 17 months,

00:19:27.947 --> 00:19:31.410
like many others in their
own different positions,

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.190
but those efforts were devoted

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:38.190
to enhancing American
national security as I saw it.

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:40.100
And I don't regret it for a minute.

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:41.377
And yet all you have written about him,

00:19:41.377 --> 00:19:43.120
all you have said about him,

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:44.300
even what you've said in this interview

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:46.830
about where the Ukrainian war would be now

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:49.319
with the Russians in
Kiev, if he was in charge,

00:19:49.319 --> 00:19:50.860
you were part of that.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.830
You were aiding and
abetting that administration

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:54.497
that you so loathe.

00:19:54.497 --> 00:19:56.390
Was that your opinion by the way,

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:58.270
or were you just reflecting other opinions

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:00.170
when you said I was
aiding and abetting Trump,

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:01.370
just out of curiosity?

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:02.203
Listen, let me explain.

00:20:02.203 --> 00:20:04.280
Well, by dint of working for him.

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.113
Yeah, that's right.

00:20:05.113 --> 00:20:07.386
You aid and abet your boss, don't you?

00:20:07.386 --> 00:20:11.110
The conclusion of your
argument is that nobody

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:13.400
with any responsibility
should have worked for Trump.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:14.810
And I reject that.

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:18.250
I think I'm not speaking for myself alone

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:20.040
because there were hundreds of people

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.690
who joined the Trump administration
to do the right thing

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:23.550
for the country.

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:26.597
And I think many of them
were cruelly disappointed

00:20:26.597 --> 00:20:28.971
at the behavior of the President,

00:20:28.971 --> 00:20:33.560
but that should not sully
the efforts that all

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:37.960
of those people made on
behalf of public service

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:38.930
for the United States.

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:40.510
And for the party, the Republican party,

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:42.230
I presume from your perspective,
you care deeply about that.

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:45.020
Yes, that's part of
politics, you're right.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:46.560
That's part of politics, isn't it?

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:49.130
Is there any country in the
world, any democratic society

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:50.780
in the world that you're aware

00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:52.570
of that doesn't have party politics?

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:55.600
I'd like to hear it if that's your view.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:56.520
That wasn't an attack,

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.650
it was a statement, that is part of it.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:01.020
You do it because you also
love your political party.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:01.860
It's a statement.

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:03.540
For philosophy, that's correct.

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:04.470
That's exactly right.

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:06.610
And what I was gonna
come onto with that is,

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:09.030
are you now politically homeless?

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:10.470
No, I don't think I'm homeless at all.

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:12.780
I have a PAC, a Super PAC, a foundation,

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:14.350
I'm actively involved in trying

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:17.360
to help candidates get elected who believe

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:19.650
in a strong US foreign policy.

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:21.400
I think the influence of Trump

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:24.600
within the party is
declining significantly.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:29.050
I expect it to continue to
decline and I expect by a return,

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:30.946
certainly in the national security field,

00:21:30.946 --> 00:21:34.746
to a Reaganite foreign policy
will be a great success

00:21:34.746 --> 00:21:36.167
philosophically and for the country.

00:21:36.167 --> 00:21:38.520
Do you think though, he
will run again, of course,

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:41.547
70 million plus votes, huge donations,

00:21:41.547 --> 00:21:45.230
some have a very opposite
view of you, of his influence?

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:47.020
Well, a lot of people have a lot of views.

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:48.890
I personally don't think Trump will run.

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:52.770
I think he knows that he lost in 2020.

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:55.697
He won't say it publicly, but
I think he knows that he lost.

00:21:55.697 --> 00:21:59.210
And I think he fears losing in 2024.

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:02.340
And one of the worst
things in Trump's view is

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:03.880
to be called a loser.

00:22:03.880 --> 00:22:07.470
So I think he will talk
incessantly about running up

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:10.860
until the last minute and then
he will announce he's not.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:12.470
And he'll try and be a king maker

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:16.584
in the Republican party
nomination fight for 2024.

00:22:16.584 --> 00:22:18.630
And from your perspective,

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:22.060
who is the bright star
in the Republican party?

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:23.760
Who should that go to?

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.240
Well, I don't have a
preferred candidate yet.

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:28.550
I think there's gonna
be a very large number

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:30.890
of people contesting for the nomination

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:33.080
and I'll watch it as it unfolds.

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:35.630
Okay, I didn't know if
you had any early people

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:38.240
you'd spotted that you
wanted to flag at this point,

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:40.840
because I suppose from what
you are saying is you see Trump

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:43.060
as an anomaly within the Republican party,

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:45.240
it can recover and go forward in a way

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:47.036
that you feel you can support it.

00:22:47.036 --> 00:22:49.147
Of course, Trump is an aberration,

00:22:49.147 --> 00:22:51.130
to use a Star Wars metaphor,

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:52.910
he's a disturbance in the force.

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:55.660
This is not anything
like a permanent change.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:57.380
Well, finally, would you still get

00:22:57.380 --> 00:22:59.280
into politics if you had your time again?

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:00.840
A lot of people look at it today

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:03.480
and they're not always
sure it's the best career.

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:05.940
They're not always sure
it's the best move for them.

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:07.384
And certainly because of social media,

00:23:07.384 --> 00:23:09.790
a lot of things have got a lot more toxic,

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:10.750
I'm sure you've noticed.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:12.150
No, I would still do the same thing.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:14.194
I think the most important thing

00:23:14.194 --> 00:23:18.070
that any American can
do really is continue

00:23:18.070 --> 00:23:19.810
to protect the security of the country,

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:23.770
which remains in my view, the
last best hope of mankind.

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:25.644
John Bolton, thank you very
much for talking to me.

00:23:25.644 --> 00:23:27.130
It was a pleasure.

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:29.430
And thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:32.093
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

